=== task: predict hard
title: A trillion
code:
```python
big = 10_000 * 10_000 * 10_000
print(big)
print(len(str(big)))
print(big // 1_000_000)
print(big % 7)
```
hint: 10,000 has 4 zeros, so multiplying three of them gives 12 zeros.
hint: One trillion divided by one million is one million.
hint: For the last line: 1,000,000 % 7 is 1 (because 999,999 is 7 × 142,857), and a trillion is a million million.
right: `big` is one trillion: a 1 and 12 zeros, 13 digits in all. A trillion holds a million millions, so `big // 1_000_000` is `1000000`. And since a million leaves remainder 1 when divided by 7, a million millions does too: `1`.
wrong: 10,000 × 10,000 × 10,000 is 1 followed by 12 zeros: `1000000000000`, which is 13 characters. Dividing by a million with `//` gives `1000000`. The remainder after dividing by 7 is 1.
wrong[1_000_000_000_000\n13\n1000000\n1]: print never shows underscores: `1000000000000`.
wrong[1000000000000\n12\n1000000\n1]: Count the 1 as well as the 12 zeros: 13 characters.
wrong[1000000000000\n13\n1000000.0\n1]: `//` with two ints gives an int, so there's no `.0`.
wrong[1000000000000\n13\n1000000\n0]: 7 doesn't divide one trillion exactly: 999,999,999,999 is a multiple of 7, so 1 is left over.

=== task: parsons hard
title: Add up the digits
prompt: Arrange lines into a program that adds up the three digits of 472 and prints:
```output
13
```
Not every line is needed.
lines:
```python
n = 472
hundreds = n // 100
tens = n // 10 % 10
ones = n % 10
print(hundreds + tens + ones)
```
distractors:
```python
tens = n % 10 // 10
ones = n // 10
```
hint: `// 100` gives the first digit of a three-digit number and `% 10` the last.
hint: The middle digit: drop the last digit first (`n // 10` is 47), then keep the last digit of that (`% 10`).
hint: `n % 10 // 10` is 2 // 10, which is 0: the order of the two steps matters.
right: `472 // 100` is 4, `472 // 10 % 10` is 47 % 10 = 7, and `472 % 10` is 2. 4 + 7 + 2 = 13.
wrong: Pick out each digit: `n // 100` (4), `n // 10 % 10` (7) and `n % 10` (2), then add them. `n // 10` alone is 47, not a single digit.
